 ## Background
-Originating from the generalization of ray marching for implicit surfaces, the specific "Sphere Tracing" algorithm was popularized by Turner Whitted and later formalized by Eric Haines and others for real-time rendering [S2, S8, S14, unofficial]. The concept of using the SDF value as a safe step size relies on the Lipschitz continuity of the distance function [S1, S8, unofficial]. Termination rules evolved to handle numerical precision issues, leading to the inclusion of oscillation detection and adaptive thresholds [S1, S26, unofficial]. Adaptive step sizing in volumetric rendering was introduced to optimize cloud and atmospheric rendering, where density varies significantly [S1, unofficial].
+Sphere tracing was introduced by John Hart as a space-leaping technique [S2, unofficial]. It is also used by William Donnelly in "Per-Pixel Displacement Mapping with Distance Functions" [S2, unofficial]. The concept of using a distance field to skip empty space is foundational to efficient ray marching [S2, unofficial].
 
